{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"algorithms-basics","title":"Searching & Counting Algorithms","group":"STL Algorithms & Ranges","order":57,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>&lt;algorithm&gt;</code> has ~100 well-tested, optimised functions that work on iterator ranges. Knowing the common ones replaces most hand-written loops.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-zoom\"></i>Find &amp; count</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42};\n\n    auto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 15);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"15 at index \" &lt;&lt; (it - v.begin()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    if (std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 99) == v.end()) std::cout &lt;&lt; \"99 not found\\n\";\n\n    auto odd = std::find_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 1; });\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"first odd \" &lt;&lt; *odd &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::count(v.begin(), v.end(), 42) &lt;&lt; ' '\n              &lt;&lt; std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x &gt; 10; }) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::string s = \"mississippi\";\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::count(s.begin(), s.end(), 's') &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>15 at index 2\n99 not found\nfirst odd 15\n1 4\n4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checks\"></i>Predicates over a range</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{2, 4, 6, 7};\n    auto even = [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; };\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::all_of(v.begin(), v.end(), even)\n              &lt;&lt; std::any_of(v.begin(), v.end(), even)\n              &lt;&lt; std::none_of(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x &lt; 0; }) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto [mn, mx] = std::minmax_element(v.begin(), v.end());\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *mn &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *mx &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *std::max_element(v.begin(), v.end()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::max({3, 9, 1}) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::min(5, 2) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::clamp(15, 0, 10) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; w{2, 4, 6, 8};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::equal(v.begin(), v.begin() + 3, w.begin()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // first 3 equal?\n    auto mm = std::mismatch(v.begin(), v.end(), w.begin());\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *mm.first &lt;&lt; \" vs \" &lt;&lt; *mm.second &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>011\n2 7 7\n9 2 10\n1\n7 vs 8</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-text-scan-2\"></i>Searching for sequences</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; data{1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3, 4};\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; pat{2, 3, 4};\n    auto it = std::search(data.begin(), data.end(), pat.begin(), pat.end());\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"pattern at \" &lt;&lt; (it - data.begin()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    auto adj = std::adjacent_find(data.begin(), data.end(), [](int a, int b) { return b &lt; a; });\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"first descent at \" &lt;&lt; (adj - data.begin()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>pattern at 4\nfirst descent at 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Returns</th><th>Complexity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>find</code>, <code>find_if</code>, <code>find_if_not</code></td><td>Iterator to first match or <code>last</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>count</code>, <code>count_if</code></td><td>Number of matches</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>all_of</code>, <code>any_of</code>, <code>none_of</code></td><td><code>bool</code></td><td>O(n), short-circuits</td></tr><tr><td><code>min_element</code>, <code>max_element</code>, <code>minmax_element</code></td><td>Iterator(s)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>equal</code>, <code>mismatch</code></td><td><code>bool</code> / pair of iterators</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>search</code>, <code>find_end</code>, <code>adjacent_find</code></td><td>Iterator</td><td>O(n·m) / O(n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>binary_search</code>, <code>lower_bound</code>, …</td><td>On <strong>sorted</strong> ranges</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why prefer std algorithms over raw loops?</summary><div><p>They name the intent (<code>any_of</code> says more than a loop with a flag), they're correct and well-tested (no off-by-one), they're optimised (and some have parallel versions), and they compose with iterators and ranges. Sean Parent's advice: “no raw loops”.</p></div></details></section>"}